Title:
extLoader — Chromium Extension Deployment/Exploitation Toolkit (Unpacked Extensions)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
extLoader is a toolkit to manage, sign/spoof, and deploy unpacked Chromium-based browser extensions (Chrome/Edge and other Chromium variants), primarily in an offensive context. It automates discovery of target user browser profiles on a remote Windows host and modifies the relevant profile preferences files to force-load an extension. The workflow includes enumerating available users/browsers (“check”), generating a deterministic extension ID by adding a manifest key (“sign”) or spoofing an existing extension ID, and pushing the extension (“exploit”). It also supports creating an offline deployment bundle (“package”) and reverting changes (“restore”). It’s useful for red teams/pentesters simulating browser persistence, credential theft, or session hijacking via malicious extensions, and for defenders to understand and test detection around extension sideloading and preference tampering. The repo is tied to Synacktiv’s research on “Phantom Extension” pathways for backdooring Chrome.

Technical Focus:
- Chromium extension ID derivation (manifest `key`) and ID spoofing
- Browser profile preference tampering (Secure Preferences / Preferences)
- Remote deployment over SMB with NTLM authentication (password/hash)
- Multi-browser/user profile enumeration on Windows (SID/user mapping)
- Packaging/replay of modified preference files and rollback/restore

Use Cases:
- Deploy a malicious extension for persistence and data collection in enterprise Chrome/Edge environments
- Spoof a legitimate extension ID to blend in with existing allowlists/policies
- Red-team simulation of browser-based initial access/post-exploitation (cookies, tokens, webmail/SSO sessions)
- Blue-team validation of detections for preference-file modification and extension sideloading
- Incident response reproduction of extension-based compromise and safe rollback testing

Keywords:
Chromium, Google Chrome, Microsoft Edge, Brave, extension sideloading, unpacked extension, manifest.json, manifest key, CRX ID, extension ID spoofing, Secure Preferences, Preferences file, profile tampering, SMB, NTLM, pass-the-hash, Windows SID, persistence, Synacktiv, Phantom Extension